    body:`<p>Walk into any struggling restaurant and you'll usually find the same thing: talented cooks, bad systems. The food is good. The passion is real. But something keeps falling apart — inconsistent portions, food costs that creep up, prep that never gets done in the right order, service that unravels when one key person calls out.</p>
<p><strong>The problem isn't the cooking. The problem is the infrastructure around the cooking.</strong></p>
<h3>The System Is the Kitchen</h3>
<p>In every high-performing kitchen, the food is almost secondary to the architecture around it. Par levels that get checked every morning. A prep list that every cook works from, not just the senior ones. A waste log that gets reviewed weekly. Recipe cards with actual food cost percentages on them.</p>
<blockquote>"A kitchen without systems is just a collection of people who happen to be in the same room."</blockquote>
<p>These aren't glamorous things. They don't make for great Instagram content. But they're the difference between a kitchen that works and one that slowly hemorrhages margin until it closes.</p>
<h3>What Systems Actually Look Like</h3>
<p>A system isn't complicated software. It's a daily checklist your team actually uses. It's a prep list that gets built the night before and reviewed at the start of each shift. It's a food cost calculation you run on every new menu item before it goes on the menu — not after.</p>
<ul>
<li>A par level system that prevents both over-ordering and running out</li>
<li>A waste log that makes waste visible and therefore manageable</li>
<li>Recipe costing that gets updated when ingredient costs change</li>
<li>A shift debrief, even a short one, where problems get named</li>
</ul>
<h3>Start Small. Be Consistent.</h3>
<p>You don't need to overhaul everything at once. Pick one system. Build a prep list template and use it for two weeks. Run your food cost numbers on your top five menu items. Log waste for one week and see what you find.</p>
<p><strong>Small systems, applied consistently, compound over time.</strong> That's how kitchens stop just surviving and start building something that lasts.</p>`,
