    id:'numbers',tag:'Operations',title:'The 6 Numbers Every Kitchen Operator Should Know',date:'April 2026',readTime:'5 min read',
    excerpt:'Most kitchen operators cook by feel. The ones who last cook by numbers. Here are the six metrics that actually tell you how your kitchen is performing.',
    body:`<p>You can have the best food in the city and still go out of business. It happens constantly. The reason is almost always the same — the operator didn't know their numbers until it was too late to fix them.</p>
<p><strong>Running a kitchen without tracking these numbers is like driving without a dashboard.</strong> You might get where you're going, but you won't know how close to empty you are until you stop.</p>
<h3>1. Food Cost Percentage</h3>
<p>Your food cost % is the cost of your ingredients divided by the revenue they generate. Fine dining targets 25–28%. Casual sits at 28–32%. Bar and pub concepts run 32–38%. If you don't know your number, you can't manage it.</p>
<h3>2. Prime Cost</h3>
<p>Prime cost is food cost plus labor cost, expressed as a percentage of revenue. <strong>The industry benchmark is 55–65%.</strong> Above 65% and you're likely not profitable regardless of your revenue. This is the single most important number in your operation.</p>
<h3>3. Cost Per Portion</h3>
<p>Every dish on your menu has a real cost to produce. Recipe costing — adding up every ingredient at its actual purchase price — gives you this number. If you don't know your cost per portion, you can't price intelligently or identify where margin is leaking.</p>
<h3>4. Waste Cost</h3>
<p>What does your kitchen throw away in a week? Most operators have no idea. Log it for one week and you'll find your waste cost — and almost always identify a fixable problem immediately. Trim waste, and food cost drops without changing a single recipe.</p>
<h3>5. Menu Item Contribution Margin</h3>
<p>Not all menu items are equal. Your highest-selling item might also be your lowest margin item. Contribution margin (menu price minus food cost) tells you which items are actually making you money, not just selling well.</p>
<h3>6. Variance</h3>
<p><strong>Variance is the difference between what your food cost should be based on sales and what it actually is.</strong> High variance means something is wrong — over-portioning, theft, waste, or incorrect recipe cards. Tracking variance weekly catches problems before they become crises.</p>
<blockquote>"Know your numbers like you know your mise en place. Every day. Every shift."</blockquote>
<p>Start with food cost percentage and prime cost. Get those two dialed in and you'll have more financial clarity than 80% of the kitchens you've ever worked in.</p>`,
