    id:'preplist',tag:'Systems',title:'Building a Prep List That Actually Works',date:'March 2026',readTime:'3 min read',
    excerpt:'Most prep lists are written on the back of a ticket or shouted across the kitchen. Here\'s how to build one that runs your service instead of reacting to it.',
    body:`<p>The prep list is the most underrated document in a professional kitchen. Done right, it's the difference between a calm, controlled service and a three-hour scramble that ends with 86'd items and an exhausted crew.</p>
<p>Done wrong — or not done at all — it means whoever shows up first decides what gets made, and service gets built on guesswork.</p>
<h3>The Problem With Most Prep Lists</h3>
<p>Most prep lists are reactive. They're written based on what's missing rather than what's needed. Someone shows up to work, opens the walk-in, and starts prepping whatever looks low. That's not a system — that's organized chaos.</p>
<p><strong>A real prep list is built the night before</strong>, based on reservation counts, historical sales data, and what's already in the house. It assigns tasks to stations, sets quantities, and gives every cook a clear target before they pick up a knife.</p>
<h3>What a Good Prep List Includes</h3>
<ul>
<li>The date and service (lunch, dinner, brunch, all-day)</li>
<li>Every task that needs to happen, not just the obvious ones</li>
<li>The station responsible for each task</li>
<li>Quantities — actual numbers, not "some" or "enough"</li>
<li>A priority order so the most critical items happen first</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Discipline Behind the List</h3>
<p>The list only works if the culture supports it. That means reviewing it at the start of each shift, not just handing it out. It means updating it as things get completed. And it means holding people accountable to it — if something on the list doesn't get done, that's a conversation, not an assumption.</p>
<blockquote>"A prep list is a promise you make to your service team. The kitchen that keeps that promise runs better than the one that doesn't."</blockquote>
<p>Use our Prep List Generator to build one you can print and take into service. Start using it every day for two weeks. Watch what changes.</p>`,
