    id:'bakers',tag:'Craft',title:"What Baker's Percentage Can Teach You About Any Recipe",date:'March 2026',readTime:'4 min read',
    excerpt:"Baker's percentage isn't just for bread bakers. It's a framework for thinking about ratios, balance, and what a recipe actually is — that applies everywhere in a professional kitchen.",
    body:`<p>If you've worked in a bakery or with a serious bread program, you know baker's percentage. Everything is expressed as a ratio relative to flour weight. Flour is always 100%. Water might be 75%. Salt is 2%. Starter is 20%.</p>
<p>At first it seems like a bakery-specific tool. But the thinking behind it applies to every corner of a professional kitchen.</p>
<h3>What Baker's Percentage Actually Is</h3>
<p>Baker's percentage is a way of expressing a recipe not in fixed quantities, but in ratios. When you express a recipe in baker's percentages, you can scale it to any batch size instantly. You can compare different recipes for the same item. You can identify where balance is off.</p>
<p><strong>A 70% hydration focaccia will behave differently from a 80% hydration one.</strong> Baker's percentage makes that visible immediately, where a recipe written in cups and tablespoons hides it.</p>
<h3>The Broader Lesson</h3>
<p>The principle behind baker's percentage — expressing ingredients as ratios rather than fixed quantities — applies everywhere. A beurre blanc with too much butter isn't wrong in grams, it's wrong in ratio. A vinaigrette that doesn't emulsify properly is a ratio problem.</p>
<ul>
<li>Ratios scale. Fixed quantities don't.</li>
<li>Ratios reveal balance. Fixed quantities hide it.</li>
<li>Ratios make you understand a recipe. Fixed quantities make you follow one.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Using It in Your Kitchen</h3>
<p>Start with any recipe you make repeatedly. Convert it to baker's percentage — or to a ratio system with a common base. Now you can scale it for any batch size without a calculator. You can identify what makes it work. And you can troubleshoot it when something goes wrong.</p>
<blockquote>"Understanding ratios is the difference between a cook who follows recipes and a cook who understands food."</blockquote>
<p>Use our Baker's Percentage calculator to convert any bread or dough recipe. Once you start thinking in percentages, you won't stop.</p>`,
    tools:[{label:'Kitchen Toolkit — Baker\'s %',page:'tools'}]
  },
